/* 4.0 content: IGCSE Biology 0610, Topics 11 to 16 */
X4TOPIC("bio",{
11:["You breathe about 20 000 times a day. Why can you not simply hold your breath and take in oxygen through your skin like a frog?","fig:BE_LUNGS",[["Health","Smoking and indoor smoke from charcoal braziers damage the lungs; chronic lung disease is common where people cook indoors."],["Sport","Athletes train to breathe more deeply so more oxygen reaches their muscles."],["Mining","Miners on the Copperbelt wear masks because dust damages the alveoli (silicosis)."]],[9,11,12],["What makes a good gas exchange surface?","How do we breathe in and out?","How does exercise change breathing?"]],
12:["Why do runners pant for minutes after a race, even when they are standing still?","emo:🏃‍♀️ 😮‍💨|The race is over. The breathing is not.",[["Food","Bread, chibwantu and munkoyo depend on respiration by yeast."],["Sport","Coaches plan training around oxygen debt and lactic acid build-up."],["Industry","Bioethanol from sugar cane is made by anaerobic respiration of yeast."]],[7,11,12,21],["Why does every cell need to respire?","How is aerobic respiration different from anaerobic respiration?","What is oxygen debt and how is it repaid?"]],
13:["Why does a person with failed kidneys need a dialysis machine three times a week to stay alive?","fig:BF_B1301",[["Health","Dialysis units at Lusaka’s University Teaching Hospital clean the blood of people whose kidneys have failed."],["Health","Drinking enough water and controlling blood pressure and diabetes protects the kidneys."],["Medicine","Urine tests show diabetes (glucose in urine) and kidney disease (protein in urine)."]],[9,13,14],["What is excretion and which organs do it?","How does the liver deal with excess amino acids?","How does the kidney filter blood and make urine?"]],
14:["You touch a hot pot and pull your hand away before you even feel the pain. How?","fig:BF_B1403",[["Health","Diabetes, controlled by the hormone insulin, affects many families in Zambia."],["Safety","Reflexes such as blinking and withdrawing protect us before we have time to think."],["Farming","Plant hormones are used to make cuttings root and to control weeds."]],[9,13,14,15],["How do nerves carry messages quickly?","How do hormones control the body more slowly?","How do the body and plants keep conditions just right?"]],
15:["Why does a doctor refuse to give antibiotics for a cold, even when the patient asks?","emo:💊 🦠|Antibiotics: powerful, but not for every illness.",[["Health","Antibiotic-resistant bacteria are a growing threat in hospitals worldwide, including in Zambia."],["Medicine","Antibiotics only work on bacteria; viruses need other treatments or vaccines."],["Society","Misused drugs such as alcohol and tobacco damage health and families."]],[10,15,18],["What is a drug?","How do antibiotics work, and why do some bacteria survive them?","How can we slow down antibiotic resistance?"]],
16:["A cassava stem cutting and a maize seed both grow into new plants. Why do farmers plant them so differently?","emo:🌿 🌽|A cassava cutting and a maize seed.",[["Farming","Cassava, sweet potato and sugar cane are grown from cuttings (asexual reproduction); maize and beans from seed."],["Health","Understanding the menstrual cycle, pregnancy and STIs helps young people make safe choices."],["Conservation","Bees and other pollinators are vital for many crops; fewer pollinators means smaller harvests."]],[16,17,18],["How do asexual and sexual reproduction differ?","How do flowers make seeds?","How does a human baby develop, and how are sex hormones involved?"]]
});
X4ADD("bio",{
"11.1":{c:["Topic 10 Diseases and immunity","Mucus and cilia in the airways trap pathogens. But the lungs must still let oxygen in. How?","1. Name two barriers that stop pathogens entering the body.<br>2. What gas does blood pick up in the lungs?<br>3. Predict: why are lungs not one big empty bag?",3],
h:["If your lungs were spread out flat, they would cover about half a tennis court. Why so big?","fig:BE_ALV","The lungs are divided into millions of alveoli. This gives a huge surface area for diffusion of oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out. Thin walls, a good blood supply and ventilation keep the gradients steep."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: breathing and gas exchange",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the breathing simulation in the gas exchange view.","Predict how a thicker alveolus wall would change oxygen uptake.","Change the surface area and wall thickness. Record oxygen reaching the blood.","Link each result to a feature of a good gas exchange surface."],{sim:"breathing"}],
 ["case","Case study: dust in the lungs",14,"Board only",["Read the case on the board.","Q1. Which part of the breathing system is damaged by fine dust?","Q2. Why do the workers become breathless when climbing stairs?","Q3. How do mucus and cilia normally protect the lungs? Why do they fail here?","Q4. Suggest two ways to protect workers."],{study:"Miners and stone crushers who breathe in fine rock dust for many years can develop silicosis. Tiny particles reach the alveoli, where they cause scarring. The alveolar walls become thick and stiff, and some alveoli are destroyed. Affected workers become short of breath and are more likely to catch tuberculosis.",src:"Based on WHO and ILO information on silicosis in mining",ans:["The alveoli.","Thicker walls and fewer alveoli mean a smaller surface area and longer diffusion distance, so less oxygen enters the blood.","Mucus traps particles and cilia sweep them upwards; very fine particles go too deep and there is too much dust.","Masks or respirators; water sprays to keep dust down; ventilation; regular health checks."]}]],
p:[["What makes a good gas exchange surface?","list",2,"List four features of a good gas exchange surface."],
 ["What are the parts of the breathing system?","label",2,"Label: trachea, larynx, bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli, ribs, intercostal muscles, diaphragm."],
 ["How are gases exchanged in the alveoli?","sketch",3,"Draw one alveolus beside a capillary. Show with arrows which way oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse."],
 ["How is the breathing system protected?","expl",2,"Explain how goblet cells and ciliated cells work together to keep the lungs clean."]],
s:["Gas exchange surfaces have a large surface area, thin walls, a good blood supply and good ventilation.","Air passes through the trachea, bronchi and bronchioles to the alveoli.","Oxygen diffuses from alveoli into the blood; carbon dioxide diffuses the other way.","Mucus from goblet cells traps particles and pathogens; cilia move the mucus away from the lungs."]},
"11.2":{c:["Lesson 11.1 Gas exchange","The alveoli need fresh air all the time to keep the concentration gradient steep. What moves the air in and out?","1. List two features of the alveoli.<br>2. Which gas diffuses into the blood?<br>3. Put your hand on your ribs and breathe in. Which way do they move?",3],
h:["A lime-water test: one tube had air blown into it, the other had air sucked through it. Why did one turn milky?","emo:🧪 ☁️ 🧪 💧|Breathed-out air turns limewater milky. Ordinary air does not, quickly.","Expired air contains much more carbon dioxide than inspired air (about 4% compared with 0.04%), because cells release carbon dioxide in respiration. Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: ventilation of the lungs",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the breathing simulation in the ventilation view.","Predict what the diaphragm and intercostal muscles do when you breathe in.","Run inhale and exhale slowly. Record the volume and pressure changes.","Increase the exercise level and describe what changes."],{sim:"breathing"}],
 ["data","Data task: breathing during exercise",12,"Board only (or a stopwatch)",["Each learner counts breaths per minute at rest.","Do one minute of star jumps, then count breaths per minute straight away and every minute for 4 minutes.","Record the class average on the board and plot it.","Q1. Describe the pattern. Q2. Explain why breathing rate and depth rise. Q3. Why does it stay high for a few minutes afterwards?"],{safety:"Learners with asthma or other conditions can time and record instead of exercising."}]],
p:[["How do the muscles of the chest move air in and out?","sort",3,"Put in order for breathing in: diaphragm contracts and flattens, external intercostals contract, ribs move up and out, volume increases, pressure falls, air enters."],
 ["How is expired air different from inspired air?","comp",2,"Complete a table comparing the percentage of oxygen, carbon dioxide and water vapour in inspired and expired air."],
 ["How does exercise affect breathing?","expl",2,"Explain why breathing rate and depth increase during exercise. Use carbon dioxide and the brain."]],
s:["Breathing in: the diaphragm contracts and flattens and the external intercostal muscles raise the ribs, so lung volume increases and pressure falls.","Expired air has less oxygen, more carbon dioxide and more water vapour than inspired air.","During exercise, more carbon dioxide in the blood is detected by the brain, which increases breathing rate and depth."]},
"12.1":{c:["Topic 11 Gas exchange","Oxygen reaches every cell through the blood. What do the cells actually do with it?","1. Which gas leaves the blood in the alveoli?<br>2. Why does breathing speed up during exercise?<br>3. Predict: name three things your body uses energy for while you sit still.",3],
h:["Why does bread dough rise when it is left in a warm place?","emo:🍞 ⬆️|Bread dough before and after an hour in the sun.","Yeast respires the sugar in the dough and releases carbon dioxide gas, which makes bubbles. Warmth speeds up the enzymes of respiration. Every living cell respires to release energy from food."],
a:[["case","Case study: the respirometer readings",14,"Board only",["Read the results on the board.","Q1. Why did the coloured liquid move towards the germinating seeds?","Q2. Why is soda lime needed?","Q3. What was the control, and why was it needed?","Q4. Predict the result at 35 °C."],{study:"A class set up two tubes, each with soda lime to absorb carbon dioxide. Tube A contained germinating bean seeds; tube B contained glass beads of the same volume. After 10 minutes at 25 °C, the coloured liquid in the capillary tube attached to A had moved 24 mm towards the seeds; in B it had not moved.",src:"Illustrative results from the standard respirometer experiment",ans:["The seeds used up oxygen in respiration; the carbon dioxide made was absorbed, so the gas volume fell.","To absorb carbon dioxide so the change in volume shows oxygen uptake.","Tube B with glass beads: shows any movement is due to the living seeds, not temperature or pressure changes.","Faster movement (more than 24 mm in 10 min), because enzymes work faster, up to their optimum."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: what do we use energy for?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: list everything your body is using energy for right now.","Pairs sort the list: movement, growth, keeping warm, active transport, making proteins, nerve impulses.","Share: which uses continue even when you are asleep?"]]],
p:[["Why do all organisms need energy?","list",2,"List five uses of energy in the human body."],
 ["What is aerobic respiration?","write",2,"Write the word equation and the balanced symbol equation for aerobic respiration."],
 ["How can we investigate respiration in yeast or seeds?","pred",2,"Predict how temperature affects the rate of respiration in yeast. Explain using enzymes."]],
s:["Respiration is the chemical reactions in cells that break down nutrient molecules and release energy for metabolism.","Energy is used for muscle contraction, protein synthesis, cell division, active transport, growth, nerve impulses and keeping warm.","Aerobic respiration: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water; C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O.","Respiration is controlled by enzymes, so temperature affects its rate."]},
"12.2":{c:["Lesson 12.1 Aerobic respiration","Aerobic respiration needs oxygen. What happens when your muscles cannot get enough?","1. Write the word equation for aerobic respiration.<br>2. Where in the cell does it happen?<br>3. Predict: why do your legs ache after a sprint?",3],
h:["Why does a sprinter keep breathing hard for several minutes after finishing a 400 m race?","emo:🏃 ⏱️ 😮‍💨|The race lasted 50 seconds. The panting lasts 5 minutes.","During the sprint the muscles could not get enough oxygen, so they respired anaerobically and made lactic acid. After the race the body needs extra oxygen to break down the lactic acid: this is the oxygen debt."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: oxygen debt",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the oxygen debt simulation.","Predict how the oxygen uptake changes before, during and after hard exercise.","Run light exercise, then a sprint. Compare the curves.","Explain the shaded area after exercise."],{sim:"oxygendebt"}],
 ["case","Case study: brewing munkoyo",12,"Board only",["Read the description of traditional munkoyo on the board.","Q1. Which organisms carry out the fermentation?","Q2. Why is the pot covered?","Q3. If left too long, the drink becomes alcoholic. Explain why.","Q4. Write the word equation for anaerobic respiration in yeast."],{study:"Munkoyo is a traditional drink made in many parts of Zambia. Maize porridge is mixed with pounded roots of the munkoyo plant, which contain enzymes that break starch into sugars. The pot is covered and left in a warm place for a day or two. Microorganisms from the roots and the air ferment the sugars, making the drink slightly sour and fizzy.",src:"Based on published studies of munkoyo, a traditional Zambian fermented beverage",ans:["Microorganisms: lactic acid bacteria and yeasts.","To keep out oxygen (and dirt), so anaerobic respiration happens.","Yeast respires anaerobically and makes alcohol (ethanol) and carbon dioxide.","glucose → alcohol (ethanol) + carbon dioxide."]}]],
p:[["What is anaerobic respiration?","write",1,"Define anaerobic respiration. How is its energy release different from aerobic respiration?"],
 ["What does yeast make when it respires without oxygen?","write",2,"Write the word and balanced equations for anaerobic respiration in yeast."],
 ["What happens in muscles during hard exercise?","write",1,"Write the word equation for anaerobic respiration in muscles."],
 ["What is oxygen debt and how is it repaid?","expl",3,"Explain why breathing and heart rate stay high after exercise. Use lactic acid, liver and aerobic respiration."]],
s:["Anaerobic respiration releases much less energy per glucose molecule than aerobic respiration.","In yeast: glucose → alcohol + carbon dioxide (C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂).","In muscles: glucose → lactic acid.","Lactic acid builds up in muscles and blood; the oxygen debt is the extra oxygen needed to break it down by aerobic respiration in the liver."]},
"13.1":{c:["Topic 12 Respiration","Respiration makes carbon dioxide and water. Breaking down proteins makes something more dangerous. What happens to it?","1. Name the waste gas of respiration.<br>2. Which organ removes it?<br>3. Predict: why is urine yellow?",3],
h:["Why is a person who eats a lot of meat and fish producing more urea?","fig:BF_B1306","Excess amino acids cannot be stored. The liver removes their nitrogen-containing part (deamination) to make urea, which is toxic in large amounts. The kidneys excrete the urea in urine."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the kidney at work",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the nephron simulation in the whole-system view.","Predict what happens to urine volume when you drink a lot of water.","Increase protein in the diet and watch the urea level.","Record two changes and explain each."],{sim:"nephron"}],
 ["disc","Discussion: what counts as excretion?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: is sweating excretion? Is passing faeces excretion? Is breathing out excretion?","Pairs decide using the definition of excretion.","Share and agree an answer for each."]]],
p:[["Which organs make up the excretory system?","label",2,"Label: kidneys, ureters, bladder, urethra, and name the lungs and liver as other excretory organs."],
 ["What is excretion?","write",1,"Write a definition of excretion and give three substances excreted by humans."],
 ["What does each part of the urinary system do?","sort",2,"Match each part to its job: kidney, ureter, bladder, urethra."],
 ["What is deamination?","expl",2,"Explain what the liver does with excess amino acids. Name the product."],
 ["Why is urea toxic?","think",1,"Explain why urea must be removed from the blood and not allowed to build up."]],
s:["Excretion is the removal of waste products of metabolism, toxic materials and substances in excess.","The kidneys filter blood; urine passes along the ureters to the bladder and out through the urethra.","The lungs excrete carbon dioxide; the kidneys excrete urea, excess water and ions.","Deamination in the liver removes the nitrogen part of excess amino acids to form urea."]},
"13.2":{c:["Lesson 13.1 Excretion and the liver","Urea travels in the blood to the kidneys. How does the kidney take out urea but keep glucose?","1. Name the organ that makes urea.<br>2. Which tube carries urine from kidney to bladder?<br>3. Predict: should a healthy person have glucose in their urine?",3],
h:["A urine test strip shows glucose in a patient’s urine. What does this tell the doctor?","emo:🧪 🟩|Glucose in the urine: a warning sign.","Normally all glucose filtered from the blood is reabsorbed in the nephron. Glucose in the urine means the blood glucose is so high that not all of it can be reabsorbed, which is a sign of diabetes."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: inside the nephron",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the nephron simulation in the nephron view.","Predict where glucose leaves the filtrate.","Increase blood glucose and watch the urine.","Change water intake and describe how the collecting duct responds."],{sim:"nephron"}],
 ["data","Data task: plasma, filtrate and urine",12,"Board only",["Use the table on the board: concentrations of protein, glucose, urea and sodium ions in blood plasma, filtrate and urine.","Q1. Why is protein in plasma but not in the filtrate?","Q2. Why is glucose in the filtrate but not in urine?","Q3. Why is urea much more concentrated in urine than in filtrate?"],{study:"Concentration in g per 100 cm³ — plasma: protein 8.0, glucose 0.1, urea 0.03, sodium ions 0.32. Filtrate: protein 0.0, glucose 0.1, urea 0.03, sodium ions 0.32. Urine: protein 0.0, glucose 0.0, urea 2.0, sodium ions 0.35.",src:"Typical textbook values",ans:["Protein molecules are too large to pass through the filter in the glomerulus.","All glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule.","Water is reabsorbed from the filtrate, so urea becomes more concentrated."]}]],
p:[["What is the structure of the kidney?","label",2,"Label the kidney: cortex, medulla, ureter, renal artery, renal vein."],
 ["What are the parts of a nephron?","label",2,"Label the nephron: glomerulus, Bowman’s capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, collecting duct."],
 ["How does ultrafiltration work?","expl",2,"Explain which substances pass into Bowman’s capsule and which stay in the blood, and why."],
 ["Where and why is glucose reabsorbed?","pred",1,"Predict what would happen if the tubule could not reabsorb glucose."],
 ["How are water and salts reabsorbed?","expl",2,"Explain how the kidney makes concentrated urine on a hot day."]],
s:["Each kidney contains about a million nephrons.","In the glomerulus, high pressure filters small molecules (water, glucose, urea, ions) into Bowman’s capsule; proteins and cells stay in the blood.","All glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule.","Water is reabsorbed in the loop of Henle and collecting duct, so the urine contains urea, excess salts and water."]},
"14.1":{c:["Topic 13 Excretion","The kidneys keep the blood’s water and salt levels steady. To do that, the body must detect changes and respond. How do messages travel?","1. Name two substances excreted by the kidneys.<br>2. What happens to urine volume on a hot day?<br>3. Predict: how does your hand know to pull away from something hot?",3],
h:["Why does your hand pull away from a hot pot before you feel the pain?","fig:BF_B1403","It is a reflex. Receptors in the skin send impulses along a sensory neurone to the spinal cord; a relay neurone passes them straight to a motor neurone, which makes the arm muscle contract. The brain only learns about it afterwards."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the reflex arc",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the reflex simulation.","Predict the path of the impulse from receptor to effector.","Run the reflex and time each step.","Compare with a voluntary response. Which is faster, and why?"],{sim:"reflex"}],
 ["data","Data task: the ruler-drop test",12,"A 30 cm ruler per pair",["One learner holds a ruler just above their partner’s open thumb and finger.","They drop it without warning. The partner catches it as quickly as possible.","Record the distance in cm. Repeat 5 times and take the mean.","Swap roles. Then repeat while counting backwards from 100.","Q1. Did reaction time improve with practice? Q2. Why did counting slow it down? Q3. Is this a reflex or a voluntary response?"]]],
p:[["How is the mammalian nervous system organised?","label",2,"Draw a simple diagram showing the central nervous system and the peripheral nervous system."],
 ["What are the three types of neurone?","comp",2,"Sketch a sensory, relay and motor neurone. Write where each is found."],
 ["How are voluntary and reflex responses different?","comp",2,"Give one example of each and two differences between them."],
 ["What is the path of a reflex arc?","sort",2,"Put in order: stimulus, receptor, sensory neurone, relay neurone, motor neurone, effector, response."],
 ["What is a synapse?","think",1,"Explain why an impulse can only cross a synapse in one direction."],
 ["How do neurotransmitters carry the message across a synapse?","sort",2,"Put in order the events at a synapse from the impulse arriving to a new impulse starting."]],
s:["The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord; the peripheral nervous system is the nerves.","Sensory neurones carry impulses from receptors; relay neurones in the CNS; motor neurones to effectors.","A reflex is an automatic, fast response that protects the body.","A synapse is a junction between neurones; neurotransmitters diffuse across the gap and bind to receptors, so impulses travel in one direction."]},
"14.2":{c:["Lesson 14.1 Neurones and reflexes","Receptors detect stimuli. The eye is a whole organ of receptors. How does it work?","1. Draw the path of a reflex arc.<br>2. Give one example of a reflex.<br>3. Look at your partner’s eyes, then shine a phone light near them. What happens to the pupil?",3],
h:["Why do your pupils shrink in bright sunlight and widen in a dark room?","fig:BF_B1410","This is the pupil reflex. In bright light the circular muscles of the iris contract and the pupil gets smaller, protecting the retina. In dim light the radial muscles contract, the pupil widens and more light reaches the retina."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the eye",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the eye simulation.","Change the light level and record the pupil diameter.","Move the object closer and further. Describe what the lens does.","Explain each change using the muscles involved."],{sim:"eye"}],
 ["out","Outdoor: pupils in sun and shade",10,"A hand mirror (or a partner)",["In pairs, stand in the shade for one minute. Look at your partner’s pupils.","Step into bright sunlight and watch the pupils immediately.","Step back into the shade. Record what happens.","Back in class, explain each change using the reflex arc."],{safety:"Never look directly at the Sun."}]],
p:[["What are sense organs?","list",1,"Name five sense organs and the stimulus each detects."],
 ["What does each part of the eye do?","label",3,"Label the eye: cornea, iris, pupil, lens, retina, fovea, optic nerve, ciliary muscle, suspensory ligaments."],
 ["What is the pupil reflex?","sort",2,"Write the reflex arc for the pupil reflex in bright light."],
 ["How do antagonistic muscles in the iris work?","comp",2,"Compare what the circular and radial muscles do in bright and dim light."],
 ["How does the eye focus on near and distant objects?","pred",2,"Predict the shape of the lens when reading a book and when looking at a distant tree."],
 ["What do rods and cones do?","comp",2,"Compare rods and cones: what they detect, where they are found."]],
s:["Sense organs contain groups of receptor cells that respond to specific stimuli.","Light is focused on the retina by the cornea and lens; the optic nerve carries impulses to the brain.","The pupil reflex controls how much light enters: circular and radial muscles of the iris are antagonistic.","Accommodation: for near objects the ciliary muscles contract, ligaments slacken and the lens becomes fatter.","Rods work in dim light; cones detect colour and are concentrated in the fovea."]},
"14.3":{c:["Lesson 14.2 The eye","Nerves send fast electrical messages. Is there another way the body sends messages, more slowly?","1. Name the coloured part of the eye.<br>2. What does the pupil reflex protect?<br>3. Predict: why does your heart pound before an exam?",3],
h:["Why does your heart pound, your mouth go dry and your hands sweat just before an exam?","emo:📝 💓|Five minutes before the exam starts.","Your adrenal glands release adrenaline into the blood. It travels to many organs: heart rate and breathing increase, and glucose is released from the liver, preparing the body for ‘fight or flight’."],
a:[["cards","Card activity: nerve or hormone?",15,"Printed cards (1 set per group)",["Lay out the two heading cards: nervous control and hormonal control.","Sort each card under the right heading.","Add one example of each type of control from your own life.","Check with another group."],{cards:[["Electrical impulses","Nervous"],["Chemical carried in the blood","Hormonal"],["Very fast response","Nervous"],["Slower response","Hormonal"],["Short-lived effect","Nervous"],["Longer-lasting effect","Hormonal"],["Acts on a precise area","Nervous"],["Can affect many organs","Hormonal"],["Pulling your hand from a flame","Nervous"],["Controlling blood glucose","Hormonal"]],cats:["Nervous","Hormonal"]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: the scary moment",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: describe a moment when you were very frightened.","List the changes your body went through.","Link each change to an effect of adrenaline."]]],
p:[["What is a hormone?","write",1,"Write a definition of a hormone."],
 ["How do hormones reach their target organs?","think",1,"Explain why a hormone made in the neck can affect cells in the foot."],
 ["Which glands make which hormones?","label",3,"Label the glands: adrenal, pancreas, testes, ovaries. Name one hormone each makes."],
 ["How are nervous and hormonal control different?","comp",2,"Make a table comparing speed, how the message travels and how long the effect lasts."],
 ["What does adrenaline do?","list",2,"List three effects of adrenaline and how each helps in fight or flight."],
 ["How does adrenaline raise blood glucose?","expl",2,"Explain how adrenaline increases the glucose available to muscles."]],
s:["A hormone is a chemical substance made by a gland, carried by the blood, that alters the activity of target organs.","Adrenal glands make adrenaline; the pancreas makes insulin; testes make testosterone; ovaries make oestrogen.","Nervous control is fast and short-lived; hormonal control is slower and longer-lasting.","Adrenaline increases heart rate, breathing rate and blood glucose, and widens pupils."]},
"14.4":{c:["Lesson 14.3 Hormones","Hormones carry slow messages. One of them keeps your blood glucose steady after a big plate of nshima. Which?","1. Define a hormone.<br>2. Which organ makes insulin?<br>3. Predict: what happens to blood glucose after a meal?",3],
h:["Why does a person with type 1 diabetes test their blood and inject insulin several times a day?","fig:BF_B1419","Their pancreas does not make insulin, so blood glucose rises too high after meals. Insulin makes the liver and muscles store glucose as glycogen. Testing shows how much insulin to inject to keep glucose in a safe range."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: blood glucose control",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the glucose simulation.","Predict the blood glucose curve after a meal for a healthy person.","Switch on ‘no insulin’ (type 1 diabetes) and repeat.","Add an insulin injection. Describe the effect and the risk of too much."],{sim:"glucose"}],
 ["diag","Diagnosis: the always-thirsty learner",14,"Board only",["Read the case: a 14-year-old is always thirsty, passes a lot of urine, is losing weight and feels tired. A urine test shows glucose.","Q1. Suggest a diagnosis. Give two pieces of evidence.","Q2. Why is there glucose in the urine?","Q3. Why is she losing weight even though she eats normally?","Q4. How is the condition treated?"],{ans:["Type 1 diabetes: high thirst, much urine, glucose in urine, weight loss.","Blood glucose is so high that the kidney cannot reabsorb all of it.","Cells cannot take up glucose without insulin, so fat and protein are broken down for energy.","Insulin injections, blood glucose monitoring, and a controlled diet with regular meals."]}]],
p:[["What is homeostasis and what does insulin do?","write",2,"Define homeostasis. Write what insulin does to blood glucose."],
 ["What is negative feedback?","expl",2,"Use the example of blood glucose to explain negative feedback in four steps."],
 ["How is blood glucose controlled?","sort",3,"Draw a flow chart: blood glucose rises → pancreas releases insulin → liver stores glycogen → blood glucose falls. Add glucagon for when glucose falls."],
 ["What is type 1 diabetes and how is it treated?","list",2,"List three symptoms of type 1 diabetes and two parts of its treatment."]],
s:["Homeostasis is the maintenance of a constant internal environment.","Negative feedback: a change from the set point triggers a response that reverses the change.","High blood glucose: insulin is released and the liver converts glucose to glycogen. Low blood glucose: glucagon is released and glycogen is broken down to glucose.","Type 1 diabetes: the pancreas does not make insulin; treated with insulin injections and a controlled diet."]},
"14.5":{c:["Lesson 14.4 Blood glucose","Blood glucose is kept steady by negative feedback. Body temperature is kept near 37 °C in the same way. What are the effectors?","1. Define negative feedback.<br>2. Which hormone lowers blood glucose?<br>3. Predict: why do your cheeks go red when you run?",3],
h:["Why do you go red and sweaty when you exercise, but pale and shivery on a cold June morning?","fig:BF_B1421","The hypothalamus detects blood temperature. When you are hot, arterioles in the skin dilate (vasodilation) and sweat glands release sweat; when you are cold, arterioles constrict (vasoconstriction) and muscles shiver to release heat. Both keep the core near 37 °C."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: temperature control",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the thermoregulation simulation.","Raise the air temperature and record skin blood flow and sweating.","Lower it and record shivering and skin blood flow.","Explain each response in terms of heat gain or heat loss."],{sim:"thermoregulation"}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the hot bus journey",10,"Board only",["A learner travels for six hours by bus from Lusaka to Livingstone in October. The bus is crowded and the windows are shut.","Q1. Describe how her body loses heat.","Q2. Why might she feel dizzy and very thirsty by the end?","Q3. Suggest three things she should do to stay safe."]]],
p:[["What part does the skin play in homeostasis?","label",2,"Label the skin diagram: sweat gland, hair, arteriole, capillaries, fat layer, receptors."],
 ["How is body temperature regulated?","sort",2,"Write the negative feedback loop for temperature: receptor, coordinator, effectors, response."],
 ["What is vasoconstriction?","expl",2,"Explain how narrowing arterioles in the skin keeps heat inside the body."],
 ["What is vasodilation?","expl",2,"Explain how widening arterioles helps the body lose heat."]],
s:["The skin contains sweat glands, arterioles, capillaries, fat and receptors that help control temperature.","The hypothalamus in the brain monitors blood temperature and coordinates responses.","Too hot: vasodilation, sweating; too cold: vasoconstriction, shivering, less sweating.","Shivering releases heat by respiration in muscles."]},
"14.6":{c:["Lesson 14.5 Temperature control","Humans use nerves and hormones to respond. Plants have no nerves. How does a seedling on a windowsill bend towards the light?","1. Name the part of the brain that controls body temperature.<br>2. Give one response to being too hot.<br>3. Predict: if you plant a bean upside down, which way will its root grow?",3],
h:["Why do bean seedlings on a windowsill all lean towards the window?","fig:BF_B1424","Shoots show positive phototropism. Auxin, a plant hormone made in the shoot tip, moves to the shaded side and makes those cells grow longer, so the shoot bends towards the light, where it can photosynthesise better."],
a:[["case","Case study: Darwin’s seedlings",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. Which seedlings bent towards the light?","Q2. What did the experiment show about where light is detected?","Q3. Why did Darwin use several seedlings for each treatment?","Q4. Later scientists found the ‘influence’ was a chemical. Name it."],{study:"In 1880 Charles Darwin and his son Francis grew grass seedlings lit from one side. Seedlings with nothing done to them bent towards the light. Seedlings with the tip cut off did not bend. Seedlings with an opaque cap over the tip did not bend. Seedlings with a clear cap over the tip bent towards the light. They concluded that ‘some influence’ moves from the tip to the part below.",src:"Retold from Darwin and Darwin, The Power of Movement in Plants (1880)",ans:["The untreated seedlings and those with a clear cap.","Light is detected by the tip.","To make the results more reliable and spot anomalies.","Auxin."]}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the seedling on the turntable",10,"Board only",["A seedling is grown in a box with a hole on one side, but the box is slowly turned on a turntable all the time.","Predict how the shoot will grow, and explain.","What would a gardener do with house plants on a windowsill to keep them straight?"]]],
p:[["What are gravitropism and phototropism?","write",2,"Define gravitropism and phototropism and say whether roots and shoots are positive or negative for each."],
 ["How can we investigate phototropism?","list",2,"Describe how to set up an experiment with seedlings lit from one side. Name the control."],
 ["How can we investigate gravitropism?","pred",2,"A bean seedling is pinned horizontally in a dark box. Predict how the root and shoot grow after 2 days."],
 ["What is auxin and where is it made?","think",1,"Where is auxin made, and how does it move?"],
 ["How does auxin cause phototropism?","sort",2,"Put in order: light from one side, auxin moves to shaded side, shaded cells elongate more, shoot bends towards light."],
 ["How does auxin cause gravitropism?","expl",2,"Explain why auxin makes shoots grow upwards but roots grow downwards."]],
s:["Gravitropism is a growth response to gravity; phototropism is a growth response to light.","Shoots are positively phototropic and negatively gravitropic; roots are positively gravitropic.","Auxin is made in the shoot tip and diffuses through the plant.","In a shoot, auxin gathers on the shaded side and stimulates cell elongation there, so the shoot bends towards the light."]},
"15.1":{c:["Topic 14 Coordination and response","Hormones and nerves control the body. Some chemicals from outside the body change how it works too. What are they?","1. Name one hormone and its effect.<br>2. How does the immune system fight bacteria (Topic 10)?<br>3. Predict: why are antibiotics not given for flu?",3],
h:["A patient stopped taking her antibiotics after 3 days because she felt better. A week later she was ill again, and the same antibiotic no longer worked. Why?","emo:💊 ✋ 🦠|Course stopped early. Infection back, and harder to treat.","Stopping early leaves the most resistant bacteria alive. They multiply, and the new population is resistant to that antibiotic. This is natural selection in action, and it is why the full course must be taken."],
a:[["data","Data task: the antibiotic discs",14,"Board only",["Use the diagram on the board: a Petri dish of bacteria with paper discs of four antibiotics, A to D.","Clear zones around the discs: A 18 mm, B 0 mm, C 9 mm, D 25 mm.","Q1. Which antibiotic is the most effective? Which is useless against this bacterium?","Q2. Why does a clear zone form?","Q3. Why would a doctor test this before treating a serious infection?"],{src:"Illustrative results of a standard antibiotic sensitivity test",ans:["D most effective; B has no effect (the bacteria are resistant).","The antibiotic diffuses into the agar and kills or stops the bacteria growing.","To choose an antibiotic that works and avoid using ones the bacteria resist."]}],
 ["debate","Debate: should antibiotics be sold without a prescription?",12,"None",["Divide the class into two sides.","Each side has 3 minutes to plan two arguments with evidence.","Each speaker has 1 minute.","After the debate, each learner writes their own view and one reason, using the word resistance."]]],
p:[["What is a drug?","write",1,"Define a drug and give one medicinal and one misused example."],
 ["How do antibiotics work, and why do they not kill viruses?","expl",2,"Explain why an antibiotic can kill bacteria but cannot affect a virus."],
 ["How do bacteria become resistant to antibiotics?","sort",2,"Put in order: a mutation gives resistance, antibiotic kills non-resistant bacteria, resistant bacteria survive, they reproduce, population is resistant."],
 ["How can we prevent antibiotic resistance?","list",2,"List three ways to reduce the development of resistant bacteria."],
 ["How do misused drugs affect health?","think",2,"Choose alcohol or tobacco. Give two effects on the body and one effect on families or society."]],
s:["A drug is any substance taken into the body that modifies or affects chemical reactions in the body.","Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop their growth, but do not affect viruses.","Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, so resistance spreads (natural selection).","Using antibiotics only when needed and completing the course reduces resistance (for example MRSA)."]},
"16.1":{c:["Topic 15 Drugs","Bacteria divide in two and quickly become a large population with the same genes. Is that the only way to reproduce?","1. How do bacteria become resistant?<br>2. Name one antibiotic.<br>3. Predict: will a cassava plant grown from a cutting look like its parent?",3],
h:["A farmer plants 1000 cassava cuttings from one excellent plant. A disease arrives and kills them all. Why did none survive?","emo:🌿 🌿 🌿 ☠️|Every cutting from the same parent plant.","Cuttings are made by asexual reproduction, so every plant is genetically identical. If one is not resistant to the disease, none is. Sexual reproduction produces variation, so some offspring may survive."],
a:[["case","Case study: cassava mosaic disease",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. Why are cassava plants in a field usually genetically identical?","Q2. Why can a virus spread so quickly through such a field?","Q3. How do plant breeders produce resistant varieties? Which type of reproduction do they use?","Q4. Give one advantage of growing cassava from cuttings."],{study:"Cassava is a staple food in Luapula and Northern provinces. Farmers grow it from stem cuttings. Cassava mosaic disease, caused by a virus spread by whiteflies, makes the leaves yellow and twisted and can cut yields by more than half. Researchers in Zambia have bred and released resistant varieties, which farmers then multiply by cuttings.",src:"Based on reports from the Zambia Agriculture Research Institute and IITA on cassava mosaic disease",ans:["They are all grown from cuttings of the same parent plants (asexual reproduction).","If one plant is not resistant, none is; whiteflies carry the virus from plant to plant.","By crossing plants (sexual reproduction) and selecting resistant offspring.","Quick, cheap, and offspring keep the good features of the parent."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: one parent or two?",8,"None",["Think, pair, share: list two advantages of asexual reproduction for a farmer.","List two advantages of sexual reproduction for a wild species.","Share and agree one situation where each is best."]]],
p:[["What is asexual reproduction?","write",1,"Define asexual reproduction and say why the offspring are identical."],
 ["How do plants reproduce asexually?","list",2,"Give three examples of asexual reproduction in plants grown in Zambia."],
 ["What are the advantages and disadvantages of asexual reproduction?","comp",2,"Make a two-column table with two advantages and two disadvantages."],
 ["What happens in sexual reproduction?","write",2,"Define sexual reproduction using gamete, fertilisation and zygote."],
 ["Why are gametes haploid?","calc",1,"Human body cells have 46 chromosomes. How many does a sperm cell have? Why must this be so?"],
 ["What are the advantages and disadvantages of sexual reproduction?","comp",2,"Give two advantages and two disadvantages of sexual reproduction for a population."]],
s:["Asexual reproduction produces genetically identical offspring from one parent.","Examples in plants: cassava and sugar cane cuttings, potato tubers, runners.","Sexual reproduction: two haploid gametes fuse at fertilisation to form a diploid zygote.","Sexual reproduction gives variation, which helps populations adapt to change."]},
"16.2":{c:["Lesson 16.1 Asexual and sexual reproduction","Sexual reproduction needs a male gamete to reach a female gamete. Plants cannot walk. How do they do it?","1. Define fertilisation.<br>2. Are gametes haploid or diploid?<br>3. Predict: why are many flowers brightly coloured and scented?",3],
h:["Why are maize flowers dull and feathery, but sunflowers large and bright?","fig:BG_B1606","Maize is wind-pollinated: it has no need to attract insects, so it has dangling anthers and feathery stigmas that catch pollen from the air. Sunflowers are insect-pollinated: bright petals, scent and nectar attract insects, which carry sticky pollen from flower to flower."],
a:[["out","Outdoor: flower detectives",15,"A hand lens if available; paper and pencil",["In pairs, find two different flowers in the school grounds (or use flowers brought from home).","Sketch each flower and label the petals, anthers and stigma.","Decide: insect-pollinated or wind-pollinated? Give three pieces of evidence.","Watch one flower for 2 minutes. Record any insects that visit."],{safety:"Do not pick flowers from protected gardens; watch out for bees."}],
 ["prob","Problem solving: the empty maize cobs",12,"Board only",["A farmer’s maize cobs have many missing grains. The tassels released pollen during a week of heavy rain and no wind.","Q1. Explain why grains are missing.","Q2. Which part of the flower failed to receive pollen?","Q3. Suggest why farmers plant maize in blocks rather than single long rows."]]],
p:[["What are the parts of an insect-pollinated flower?","label",2,"Label: sepal, petal, stamen (anther, filament), carpel (stigma, style, ovary, ovule), nectary."],
 ["How are wind-pollinated flowers different?","comp",2,"Make a table comparing insect- and wind-pollinated flowers: petals, anthers, stigmas, pollen."],
 ["What is pollination, and how do pollen grains differ?","expl",2,"Explain why wind-carried pollen is light and smooth but insect pollen is sticky or spiky."],
 ["What happens after pollination to bring about fertilisation?","sort",2,"Put in order: pollen lands on stigma, pollen tube grows, nucleus moves down, fertilises ovule, seed forms."],
 ["What do seeds need to germinate?","pred",2,"Predict which seeds germinate: in dry soil, in water with no air, in moist soil at 25 °C, in moist soil at 2 °C."],
 ["Why is cross-pollination better than self-pollination?","expl",2,"Explain one advantage of cross-pollination for a species."]],
s:["Insect-pollinated flowers have large, bright petals, scent and nectar; wind-pollinated flowers have small dull petals, exposed anthers and feathery stigmas.","Pollination is the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma.","Fertilisation happens when a pollen nucleus fuses with an ovule nucleus after a pollen tube grows.","Germination needs water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.","Cross-pollination increases variation; self-pollination keeps it low."]},
"16.3":{c:["Lesson 16.2 Reproduction in plants","In plants, pollen tubes carry the male nucleus to the ovule. In humans, sperm must swim to the egg. Where does this happen?","1. Define pollination.<br>2. What is formed when an ovule is fertilised?<br>3. Predict: where in the female body does fertilisation happen?",3],
h:["A fertilised egg is smaller than a full stop. Nine months later it is a baby of over 3 kg. How does it get its food and oxygen?","fig:BG_B1614","Through the placenta. The fetus’s blood flows in the umbilical cord to the placenta, where oxygen and nutrients diffuse from the mother’s blood and wastes diffuse the other way, without the two bloods mixing."],
a:[["case","Case study: why antenatal clinics matter",14,"Board only",["Read the study on the board.","Q1. Name two substances that cross the placenta and help the fetus.","Q2. Name two harmful things that can cross the placenta.","Q3. Why are pregnant women advised not to drink alcohol or smoke?","Q4. Why are HIV tests offered at antenatal clinics?"],{study:"Zambia’s antenatal clinics check the health of pregnant women and their babies. Mothers receive iron and folic acid tablets, are tested for HIV and syphilis, and are given malaria prevention. Women living with HIV take antiretroviral medicine, which greatly reduces the chance that the virus passes to the baby.",src:"Based on Zambia Ministry of Health and WHO guidance on antenatal care",ans:["Oxygen, glucose, amino acids (also antibodies, water, ions).","Alcohol, nicotine, some pathogens such as HIV and rubella, some drugs.","They cross the placenta and can harm the development of the fetus.","Treatment reduces mother-to-child transmission of HIV."]}],
 ["disc","Discussion: from egg to baby",8,"None",["Pairs put these in order: implantation, fertilisation, birth, cell division into a ball of cells, development of organs.","Share: where in the body does each stage happen?"]]],
p:[["What are the parts of the male reproductive system?","label",2,"Label: testes, scrotum, sperm duct, prostate gland, urethra, penis. Give the job of two parts."],
 ["What are the parts of the female reproductive system?","label",2,"Label: ovaries, oviducts, uterus, cervix, vagina. Give the job of two parts."],
 ["How does fertilisation happen?","sort",2,"Put in order: sperm deposited, sperm swim through cervix and uterus, egg in oviduct, nuclei fuse, zygote divides."],
 ["How are sperm and egg cells adapted?","comp",2,"Compare a sperm cell and an egg cell: size, number made, movement, adaptations."],
 ["How does a fetus grow and develop in the uterus?","list",2,"List three things that protect and support a developing fetus."],
 ["What do the placenta and umbilical cord do?","expl",2,"Explain what crosses the placenta in each direction and why the bloods do not mix."]],
s:["Male: testes make sperm and testosterone; sperm pass along the sperm duct and urethra.","Female: ovaries release eggs; fertilisation happens in the oviduct; the embryo implants in the uterus.","Sperm have a flagellum and many mitochondria; eggs are large with energy stores and a jelly coat.","The placenta allows oxygen and nutrients to pass to the fetus and wastes to pass to the mother; the amniotic fluid protects the fetus."]},
"16.4":{c:["Lesson 16.3 Human reproduction","The uterus must be ready for an embryo at the right time each month. What controls this?","1. Where does fertilisation happen?<br>2. What does the placenta do?<br>3. Recall from Topic 14: what is a hormone?",3],
h:["Why does the uterus lining build up and break down roughly every 28 days?","fig:BG_B1619","The menstrual cycle is controlled by hormones. Oestrogen and progesterone build up the uterus lining so it can receive an embryo. If no fertilisation happens, progesterone falls and the lining breaks down (menstruation). FSH and LH from the pituitary control egg development and ovulation."],
a:[["sim","Simulation: the menstrual cycle",12,"Board or projector only",["Open the menstrual cycle simulation.","Predict which hormone peaks just before ovulation.","Run the cycle and record the day of ovulation and the levels of each hormone.","Switch on ‘pregnancy’ and describe what changes."],{sim:"menstrual"}],
 ["disc","Discussion: facts versus myths about HIV",10,"Board only",["The teacher reads six statements about HIV aloud (for example: ‘you can catch HIV by sharing a cup’).","Pairs decide: fact or myth? Give a reason using how HIV is transmitted.","Share and correct any myths.","Agree three ways to reduce the spread of HIV and other STIs."]]],
p:[["What changes happen at puberty?","list",2,"List two secondary sexual characteristics in males and two in females, and the hormone that causes them."],
 ["What happens in the menstrual cycle?","sort",2,"Put in order: menstruation, lining builds up, ovulation, lining maintained, lining breaks down."],
 ["What do FSH and LH do?","comp",2,"Write where FSH and LH are made and what each does."],
 ["How do oestrogen and progesterone work with FSH and LH?","expl",2,"Explain why progesterone stays high during pregnancy."],
 ["What are STIs, and how is HIV transmitted?","list",2,"Name two STIs and three ways HIV is transmitted."],
 ["How can the spread of STIs be controlled?","list",2,"List three ways to reduce the spread of STIs."]],
s:["Testosterone and oestrogen cause secondary sexual characteristics at puberty.","The menstrual cycle: menstruation, build-up of the lining, ovulation around day 14, then breakdown if no pregnancy.","FSH (pituitary) stimulates egg development; LH triggers ovulation; oestrogen and progesterone build and maintain the lining.","STIs, including HIV, spread through sexual contact and body fluids; condoms, testing and treatment reduce spread."]}
});
